# Contents list

Add a contents list only when the page is long enough that someone needs a route to one section. Write section names as tasks, and cut sections before you add navigation. A three-sentence card does not need one.

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation. They do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## Use the shared navigation contract

[Contents list](../../components/contents-list.html#choose-the-navigation-scope) owns the difference between “On this page” fragments and “In this guide” routes. This page keeps the article examples. Use the [mini-hub pattern](../../patterns/mini-hub.html) when several real pages also need current-topic and previous or next context.

## When to use it

Use it on substantial guidance, such as recovering from a failed backup or setting up a new heat pump. Name each section by what the reader does, for example “Restore from a backup”.

## Structure and behaviour

Generate links from stable heading IDs and keep them in the same order as the document. Label the navigation “On this page” for local anchors and “In this guide” for other pages. A current chapter is plain text. One nested level can help a technical guide. Deeper nesting is a sign the content needs restructuring.

## Home Assistant boundary

This is a documentation pattern. It is not injected into Home Assistant panels. Sticky positioning must not hide a target under the header or fill a mobile screen. Do not make a contents list look like a progress checklist.

